SELECT set_config('request.jwt.claim.role','service_role',false);
SELECT set_config('request.jwt.claim.sub','f49d6153-1835-4455-81f3-2918d5d3484e',false);
BEGIN;
INSERT INTO public.qb_questions (subject_id, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, points, tags) VALUES
('3c09b2b2-10bd-4e1e-a51d-661d013948bc', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'A production engineer calculates ''line loading'' for a mixed-style production plan: Line A is scheduled to run Style 1 (SAM=15 min, target 800 pcs) for the first half of the week, then switch to Style 2 (SAM=22 min, target 500 pcs) for the second half. If total available line capacity is 2,400 earned-minutes/day and the line runs 5 days total (2.5 days per style), does this plan fit within available capacity?', '["Style 1 requires 800×15=12,000 earned-minutes total, available over 2.5 days = 2.5×2,400=6,000 minutes — Style 1 ALONE requires DOUBLE the available capacity in its allotted time, meaning this plan does NOT fit and is significantly over capacity before even considering Style 2","Both styles fit comfortably within capacity, since the total weekly capacity (5×2,400=12,000 minutes) exactly matches Style 1''s total requirement alone, leaving Style 2 needing zero additional capacity","The plan fits easily, since SAM values only affect individual operation timing, not overall line capacity feasibility calculations","Total capacity should be calculated by ADDING both styles'' SAM values together first (15+22=37) before comparing against available minutes, a fundamentally different and incorrect calculation approach than checking each style''s requirement against its own allotted time separately"]'::jsonb, 'Style 1 requires 800×15=12,000 earned-minutes total, available over 2.5 days = 2.5×2,400=6,000 minutes — Style 1 ALONE requires DOUBLE the available capacity in its allotted time, meaning this plan does NOT fit and is significantly over capacity before even considering Style 2', 'Style 1''s total earned-minute requirement = target output × SAM = 800×15=12,000 minutes. Available capacity in its allotted 2.5 days = 2.5×2,400=6,000 minutes. Since 12,000 required minutes vastly exceeds the 6,000 available minutes, Style 1 ALONE is already double the available capacity in its scheduled timeframe — this plan is severely infeasible as structured, and a production planner discovering this BEFORE committing to the schedule (rather than after falling behind) is exactly the value of doing this capacity-feasibility calculation upfront, directly connecting to the earlier capacity-planning and delivery-date-commitment discussions.', 3, '{"line-loading-feasibility-calculation"}'::text[]),
('3c09b2b2-10bd-4e1e-a51d-661d013948bc', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'A technical designer explains ''ease of assembly'' scoring (a systematic rating of how difficult/time-consuming a garment design is to actually sew, considering factors like curve complexity, number of small pieces, and matching/alignment requirements) is sometimes used to REJECT or revise a design BEFORE it goes into costing/sampling. What business problem does catching a high-difficulty design at the DESIGN stage (rather than discovering it during costing or production) specifically prevent?', '["Ease-of-assembly scoring at the design stage has no genuine business value, since assembly difficulty only matters once actual production begins, making early-stage scoring an unnecessary extra step","Catching a design with unrealistic assembly complexity EARLY prevents the entire downstream cascade of wasted effort already established throughout this domain: an unrealistically complex design would eventually show up as either a much higher-than-expected SAM (inflating CM cost beyond what''s competitive, connecting to the earlier garment-costing discussion) or as a genuinely difficult-to-execute style causing high defect rates/rework on the production line (connecting to the earlier RFT and rework-cost discussions) — catching this at the DESIGN stage, before significant technical development/costing/sampling investment, is far cheaper than discovering it after committing resources","Assembly difficulty has no relationship to either garment costing or production quality outcomes, making ease-of-assembly scoring an irrelevant exercise disconnected from any real downstream business consequence","Ease-of-assembly scoring should only ever be applied AFTER a style is already in full bulk production, making early design-stage application technically counterproductive and poorly timed"]'::jsonb, 'Catching a design with unrealistic assembly complexity EARLY prevents the entire downstream cascade of wasted effort already established throughout this domain: an unrealistically complex design would eventually show up as either a much higher-than-expected SAM (inflating CM cost beyond what''s competitive, connecting to the earlier garment-costing discussion) or as a genuinely difficult-to-execute style causing high defect rates/rework on the production line (connecting to the earlier RFT and rework-cost discussions) — catching this at the DESIGN stage, before significant technical development/costing/sampling investment, is far cheaper than discovering it after committing resources', 'This connects the earlier ''catch problems as early as possible'' cost-escalation principle (established for IQC, in-line inspection, and tech-pack completeness) to the very BEGINNING of the product development chain — an overly complex design will eventually manifest as either an inflated SAM (making the style cost-uncompetitive once costing is done) or genuine production difficulty (high defect/rework rates once sampling/bulk production begins) — but discovering this AFTER significant technical development, costing, and sampling investment has already occurred is far more costly than catching it at the DESIGN stage itself, before that investment begins, directly extending the same early-detection cost-avoidance logic to the earliest possible point in the entire product lifecycle.', 3, '{"ease-of-assembly-early-detection"}'::text[]),
('3c09b2b2-10bd-4e1e-a51d-661d013948bc', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'A garment factory''s technical team compares two button-attachment methods for a children''s wear order: hand-sewn buttons (individually sewn, each attachment''s tightness dependent on operator skill/consistency) versus button-attaching MACHINE (automated, mechanically consistent stitch count/tension per button). Beyond raw production speed, why might the button-attaching machine be SPECIFICALLY preferred for children''s wear, connecting to the earlier safety discussions?', '["Button attachment method has no relationship to the earlier-established button pull-strength safety concern for children''s wear, making this comparison irrelevant to that specific safety requirement","A machine-based attachment method provides much more CONSISTENT stitch count/tension per button compared to hand-sewing (where individual operator skill/attention/fatigue can cause variable, sometimes-inadequate attachment strength) — since children''s wear button pull-strength is a genuine, safety-critical requirement (as established earlier, given the choking-hazard risk of a detached button), the machine''s mechanical CONSISTENCY directly reduces the risk of an individual weak/inadequately-attached button slipping through due to human variability, making it specifically valuable for this safety-critical application beyond just raw speed","Hand-sewn buttons always provide STRONGER, more reliable attachment than machine-attached buttons, the reverse of the actual consistency relationship between these two methods relevant to this safety application","Children''s wear button attachment method selection has no relationship to any safety consideration whatsoever, making the choice between hand-sewing and machine attachment purely about production speed with zero quality/safety dimension"]'::jsonb, 'A machine-based attachment method provides much more CONSISTENT stitch count/tension per button compared to hand-sewing (where individual operator skill/attention/fatigue can cause variable, sometimes-inadequate attachment strength) — since children''s wear button pull-strength is a genuine, safety-critical requirement (as established earlier, given the choking-hazard risk of a detached button), the machine''s mechanical CONSISTENCY directly reduces the risk of an individual weak/inadequately-attached button slipping through due to human variability, making it specifically valuable for this safety-critical application beyond just raw speed', 'This directly connects the earlier children''s-wear button/snap pull-strength safety discussion to a specific production-method choice — while hand-sewing CAN achieve adequate strength when done well, its attachment quality depends on individual operator skill, attention, and consistency across potentially thousands of buttons per shift (with natural human variability, fatigue, and occasional inattention risk), while a properly calibrated attachment MACHINE delivers highly consistent stitch count/tension for EVERY button — for a genuinely safety-critical requirement like children''s wear button security (where even a small percentage of inadequately-attached buttons represents real risk), this mechanical consistency advantage is a meaningful, safety-driven reason to prefer machine attachment specifically for this application, beyond the more obvious raw production-speed benefit.', 3, '{"button-attachment-safety-consistency"}'::text[]);
COMMIT;
